"Book.pdf (https://mdpi-res.com/bookfiles/book/1386/Japanese_Media_Cultures_in_Japan_and_Abroad_Transnational_Consumption_of_Manga_Anime_and_MediaMixes.pdf?v=1748566890)\nciteturn18view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 164; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn16view1\",\"lineno\":3163}); Total lines: 7219\nL3116@P75: of authorial agency, creative and cultural production, and methodological approaches to interpreting\nL3117@P75: (and consuming) anime. When considered in the context of transnational consumption, we find\nL3118@P75: the same methodological approaches common in analyzing anime as a commentary on Japanese\nL3119@P75: society by directors: anime is “Japanese culture” consumed outside of Japan, making anime works\nL3120@P75: a speech-act about Japan, because, as is commonly thought, anime comes from Japan. This produces\nL3121@P75: a conception of transnationality whereby people consuming anime outside of Japan are consuming\nL3122@P75: Japanese products, which is less transnational (across borders, operating beyond the received notions\nL3123@P75: of nation-state) and more inter-national (two distinctive nation-states engaged in cultural exchange,\nL3124@P75: Iwabuchi 2010), producing a sense of “inside-outside” that is relatively neatly defined—in this case,\nL3125@P75: consuming Japanese media (produced inside of Japan) while the consumers are outside of Japan.\nL3126@P75: Thus, there is a sharp distinction seen between production (authored by directors in Japan) and\nL3127@P75: consumption (inside vs. outside of Japan) that overlaps with the idea of the inter-national (distinctive\nL3128@P75: nation-states), which might be overcome by engaging with both production and consumption to\nL3129@P75: explore the transnational dynamics of anime.\nL3130@P75: This is not a disavowal of reading anime as a commentary on Japan or the contributions such\nL3131@P75: scholarship has made. Nor is this a denial of the importance of the nation at play here. Rather, this is\nL3132@P75: a suggestion for exploring an alternative approach that more fully engages with transnationality.\nL3133@P75: For instance, while it is widely accepted that anime is a transnationally consumed product, and\nL3134@P75: it is generally acknowledged in academia that anime has a history of transnational production,\nL3135@P75: the implications of these layers of transnationality are not often explored. This is important to\nL3136@P75: consider as much of anime’s transnational labor is for the animation, which is the basis of the very\nL3137@P75: media product itself: anime is animation, making the results of the animators’ labor a significant\nL3138@P75: portion of what we actually receive as the final work. We might connect the lack of exploration to\nL3139@P75: the above-mentioned tendency to emphasize directors (who operate in Japan) over animators (who\nL3140@P75: operate across national borders), which also relates to the framework of the inter-national rather\nL3141@P75: than the transnational. As such, it can be productive to consider a more inclusive view of anime’s\nL3142@P75: transnationality—that is, accounting for the dispersal of agency in anime production, how this extends\nL3143@P75: to transnational production, and how (and if) these elements of anime’s transnationality are engaged\nL3144@P75: with in the transnational consumption of anime.\nL3145@P75: In order to explore the dynamics of agency in anime production, in this article I will conduct\nL3146@P75: an analysis of the anime Shirobako (2014), which details a number of processes of anime production\nL3147@P75: as the central narrative of the series. Broadly, this analysis is also done in reference to Ian Condry’s\nL3148@P75: (2013) ethnographical work on the anime industry in Tokyo, which he describes as a “collaborative\nL3149@P75: creativity” that involves not just typical creative producers (animators, directors) but even corporate\nL3150@P75: executives. In terms of methodological approach, this article is underpinned by Bruno Latour’s (2005)\nL3151@P75: “actor-network theory.” I use “agency” here as an adapted version pulled from Latour’s work, where an\nL3152@P75: “agent” performs actions, “making some difference to a state of affairs, transforming some As into Bs\nL3153@P75: through trials with Cs” (pp. 52–53). Agents that exercise such capacities are not limited to human\nL3154@P75: actors but include a whole host of non-human actors that work with/on humans, including various\nL3155@P75: materials, technologies, and anime’s media-form itself—that is, the repeated patterns utilized that\nL3156@P75: distinguish anime as a particular type of media product.\nL3157@P75: Because of the relationship between the multitude of human and non-human actors, each working\nL3158@P75: with, on, and through one another, one can trace an intricate web of actions that affect the final product,\nL3159@P75: often in directly discernible ways (e.g., the narrative, the animation, etc.). As such, instead of a\nL3160@P75: single agent in one location who is the source that orchestrates the production, there is a dispersal of\nL3161@P75-76: 67Arts 2018, 7, 27\nL3162@P76: agency, a “network” of actions from multiple agents (at different times, in different places) and the\nL3163@P76: negotiations that arise from their mutual engagement that result in effects that we see in the anime\nL3164@P76: product. This does not mean that an anime work cannot sustain itself as a commentary on a topic,\nL3165@P76: or cannot be read as exploring an issue, but that the source of that exploration becomes complicated,\nL3166@P76: as does the context with which it may be engaging. As a self-reflexive exposition of anime production,\nL3167@P76: Shirobako depicts such a web of effects. In the analysis that follows, I will show that anime production\nL3168@P76: involves a large number of actors with differing degrees of control, each one involved in diverse\nL3169@P76: developments that result in a constant process of negotiation, the outcomes of which are tangible in the\nL3170@P76: final product (the anime itself) and can (and do) occur transnationally. Anime production thus operates\nL3171@P76: as a network of actors whose agency is dispersed across a chain of hierarchies, making attribution\nL3172@P76: of a single actor (human or non-human) as the agent who addresses Japan (or the world) difficult\nL3173@P76: to sustain.\nL3174@P76: Furthermore, Shirobako, as an anime, invites (if not enforces) an alternative approach to consuming\nL3175@P76: anime, less as a commentary on Japan per se, and more on the anime industry itself. The series\nL3176@P76: provides a sharp focus on the labor involved in anime production, with a particular emphasis on\nL3177@P76: the animation: much of the drama of the series is motivated by issues involving the animation\nL3178@P76: itself (though other elements, such as story-boarding, script-writing, and voice-acting are prevalent).\nL3179@P76: When these animation production issues are resolved, many times they will display the intradiegetic\nL3180@P76: animation at the end of the episode, the sakuga (a complex animated sequence), often lasting just a few\nL3181@P76: seconds, but revealing the fruits of the labor (and its conflicts) that were just witnessed as the drama\nL3182@P76: of the episode. In this way, the series forces the viewer to focus on the sakuga sequences as the product\nL3183@P76: of labor by many people. These sequences are all from the intradiegetic anime the studio is producing,\nL3184@P76: but one which we (the viewers) do not know the details of the narrative.2 This type of consumption of\nL3185@P76: the animation sequence itself, estranged from narrative, resembles the type of consumption practices\nL3186@P76: employed by a particular type of fan: the sakuga-fan.\nL3187@P76: While I have glossed sakuga above as “a complex animated sequence,” the very term “sakuga”\nL3188@P76: is slightly ambiguous in Japanese. At its most simple, it is “images that are made” and is usually\nL3189@P76: associated with key-animation (officially known by the separate term of genga—key-frames that are\nL3190@P76: not colored and do not include in-betweens) but sakuga can also mean the final cut of an animated\nL3191@P76: sequence. As such, the term implies that it is merely one part of a larger production process and\nL3192@P76: holds the potential for an ambiguity of attribution to multiple agents in its production while still\nL3193@P76: attributing credit to a key-animator. Terminology aside, it is the sakuga part of anime production that\nL3194@P76: is the central concern of sakuga-fans. They are interested in these key sequences as the focal point of\nL3195@P76: production. While not ignoring elements like narrative, character design, or voice-acting, sakuga is\nL3196@P76: what they concentrate on.\nL3197@P76: As such, in the final section, I will transition into how sakuga-fans tend to focus on form and\nL3198@P76: the materiality of the medium of animation as opposed to “Japanese society.” The products of\nL3199@P76: their consumption practices are published on the internet, making the engagement with their work\nL3200@P76: transnational, as it is available freely on the internet, across the world. I will examine how, based on\nL3201@P76: English-language YouTube and blog posts by such fans, they present an alternative type of consumption\nL3202@P76: that engages with a dispersed sense of agency regarding animation, the people involved, and how they\nL3203@P76: also examine non-Japanese animators (and thus anime’s transnationality). In fact, this focus on the\nL3204@P76: animation and its production sets up an alternative type of viewing that generally disregards narrative\nL3205@P76: in favor of the craft of animation and the people who labor behind it. Interestingly, these transnational\nL3206@P76: fans who focus on form also seem concerned (and critically engaged) with the labor of animators and\nL3207@P76: their harsh working conditions inside (and outside) Japan.\nL3208@P76: 2 Though, it should be noted, the two intradiegetic anime produced by Musashino Animation were later released as\nL3209@P76: one-episode OVAs.\nL3210@P76-77: 68Arts 2018, 7, 27\nL3211@P77: 2. Shirobako: Analysis\nL3212@P77: What makes Shirobako relatively unique in the TV anime world is how it brings the dispersal\nL3213@P77: of agency in animation production to the foreground. There are precursors to Shirobako, such as\nL3214@P77: Otaku no Video (1991), which details the transformation of a college student into an otaku who then\nL3215@P77: creates two otaku media companies, and Bakuman (2010), a series about an aspiring manga creator\nL3216@P77: and their relationship with the anime industry. However, Shirobako focuses almost entirely on the\nL3217@P77: anime industry, exploring the finer details of anime production itself. Centering around the production\nL3218@P77: assistant and administrator Aoi Miyamori, Shirobako follows her role at the fictional anime studio\nL3219@P77: Musashino Animation, and includes various characters heavily inspired by those in similar roles (in\nL3220@P77: different studios) in the real-life anime industry. In the background, Miyamori’s four friends from\nL3221@P77: her hometown have also joined the anime industry in different roles (2D key animator, 3D animator,\nL3222@P77: script writer, and voice-actress), and they dream of working on an anime together. The narrative of\nL3223@P77: the series follows the production of two different intradiegetic anime (Exodus! and The Third Girls\nL3224@P77: Aerial Squad) and the drama that occurs during the production process of TV anime. As such, Shirobako\nL3225@P77: provides a convenient object of analysis with which to examine the workings of the anime industry\nL3226@P77: and exactly who (and what) is guiding the production, making decisions, and producing effects on the\nL3227@P77: final product. However, because of the self-reflexive mode of address (an anime about making anime),\nL3228@P77: the work should be approached with a strong dose of skepticism. Its self-representation should not be\nL3229@P77: considered the final word on the status of the industry and can easily overly dramatize or misrepresent\nL3230@P77: the actualities of the work place.\nL3231@P77: Though acknowledging that this is not necessarily an accurate depiction of the anime industry,\nL3232@P77: the form of the presentation of Shirobako (an anime about making anime) constructs a self-referential\nL3233@P77: account of the anime industry while also offering some evidence for the claims about distributive\nL3234@P77: agency in anime production. Through an analysis of key sequences of the series, I will (1) discuss how\nL3235@P77: there is a production hierarchy where various roles exert different degrees of control over the creative\nL3236@P77: process; (2) explore the agency of the materials involved as the animators and directors struggle\nL3237@P77: with elements of cel-animation and its mixture with CG animation, and briefly depict the differences\nL3238@P77: between manga and anime production/industries; (3) detail how the media-form constrains the\nL3239@P77: production: different expectations for the product, the types of facial expressions used, and the structure\nL3240@P77: of the narrative (action-oriented climaxes); and (4) provide some brief information on the transnational\nL3241@P77: production involved in Shirobako—something unacknowledged in the series—and discuss how this\nL3242@P77: manner of dispersed agency in anime production frees anime to acknowledge its transnationality\nL3243@P77: rather than emphasize its exclusive nationality. For ease of explanation, these have been divided into\nL3244@P77: distinct sections. However, it is important to note that many of these are interconnected, such as when\nL3245@P77: the capabilities of the medium (cel animation) allows for a complex division of labor, which affords for\nL3246@P77: (transnational) sub-contracting, and additionally, can affect where and when certain types of animation\nL3247@P77: are employed in the narrative.\nL3248@P77: 2.1. Negotiated Decisions\nL3249@P77: Let me begin with the production hierarchy, which, as noted before, is often conceived with the\nL3250@P77: directors and top-level producers at the top of the chain of command—those making the decisions,\nL3251@P77: so to speak. Even if this is expanded, anime is so often seen from the perspective of a type of “creative\nL3252@P77: industry” in which animators, writers, designers, voice-actors, and musicians get much of the spotlight\nL3253@P77: (and credit) for a production. However, Shirobako consistently undermines this conception of the\nL3254@P77: hierarchy of decision making power in anime. In Shirobako, an administrator takes center stage\nL3255@P77: and is shown as integral to the anime production process, part and parcel of this creative industry.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Manual for TV Anime Series Production Assistants アニメシリーズ制作における-制作進行のマニュアル_英語 | 日本動画協会 (https://aja.gr.jp/download/%E3%82%A2%E3%83%8B%E3%83%A1%E3%82%B7%E3%83%AA%E3%83%BC%E3%82%BA%E5%88%B6%E4%BD%9C%E3%81%AB%E3%81%8A%E3%81%91%E3%82%8B-%E5%88%B6%E4%BD%9C%E9%80%B2%E8%A1%8C%E3%81%AE%E3%83%9E%E3%83%8B%E3%83%A5%E3%82%A2)\nciteturn18view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn16view0\",\"id\":52}); Total lines: 480\n"
